# The documented OnFailure= hook (docs/cli.md output discipline, last item).
#
# the CLI event contract asks for "a documented `OnFailure=` hook for future monitoring without
# embedding a notification provider in the application". This is that hook, and
# it is a working unit rather than a commented-out line, for the reason decision
# 59 gives about hardening checklists and SEC-001 gives about the credential
# drop-in: a control nobody has run is a document. A commented-out OnFailure=
# would be discovered to be misspelled on the night it was needed.
#
# What it does by default is the least a handler can honestly do — print the
# failed unit's own verdict into the journal, tagged and at error priority:
#
#   Id=hfcache-sync.service
#   Result=exit-code
#   ExecMainCode=1
#   ExecMainStatus=20
#   InvocationID=c0a12f5f43ea4f7281f5689724927971
#
# `ExecMainStatus` is the application's §11.2 classification, so the line already
# distinguishes a safety refusal (20) from repository failures (30) from an
# integrity failure (40). `InvocationID` is the handle for the rest of it:
#
#   journalctl _SYSTEMD_INVOCATION_ID=<id>
#
# which returns exactly that run's output and nothing else. This is why the
# journald drop-in shipped alongside it matters — before it, that command
# answered only until the next reboot.
#
# **Replacing it with a real notifier.** Drop in an override and swap ExecStart:
#
#   /etc/systemd/system/hfcache-failure@.service.d/notify.conf
#     [Service]
#     ExecStart=
#     ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/notify-somewhere %i
#
# The empty ExecStart= is required; without it systemd appends rather than
# replaces. Keep the provider here rather than in the application: hfcache has no
# opinion about how this site pages someone, and the CLI event contract is explicit that it should
# not acquire one.
#
# **Shipped as an example, not installed.** the CLI event contract asks that a hook be *left* for
# future monitoring, and this deployment has nobody to notify: a handler that
# only reprints what systemd already logged is a seam, not a control, and
# installing one would add a unit that fires on every failure to no end. The
# same reasoning as `hfcache-sync.service.d/credential.conf.example`, which
# ships complete and uninstalled against the day the archive needs a token.
#
# It has been exercised, which is what makes it an example worth having rather
# than untested prose. Against a genuinely failed `hfcache-sync.service` it
# produced five lines at `PRIORITY=3` tagged `hfcache-failure`, carrying
# `ExecMainStatus=10` — the application's own §11.2 classification, intact
# through systemd — and an `InvocationID` that resolved to exactly that run's
# output. It fired once, for the failure; the clean run after it did not
# trigger it. Decision 63 records the evidence.
#
# To activate, both halves are needed:
#
#   1. install this file, without the .example suffix:
#        install -m 0644 -o root -g root hfcache-failure@.service.example \
#          /etc/systemd/system/hfcache-failure@.service
#   2. uncomment the `OnFailure=hfcache-failure@%n.service` line in
#      hfcache-sync.service and hfcache-verify.service, reinstall them, and
#      `systemctl daemon-reload`.
#
# A template, so one handler serves every unit and names which one failed: `%n`
# in the failing unit passes that unit's name as this one's instance. It is
# never enabled — nothing wants it; it is started by the OnFailure= of a unit
# that failed.

[Unit]
Description=Report the failure of %i
Documentation=file:///opt/hfcache/current/docs/deployment-centos-stream-10.md
Documentation=file:///opt/hfcache/current/docs/operations.md

[Service]
Type=oneshot

# `systemctl show` reads; it does not act. The handler deliberately cannot
# restart, reset, or otherwise touch the unit that failed — a failure handler
# that intervenes turns one bad run into a loop, and every hfcache failure mode
# is one where stopping and leaving the evidence is correct (`AD-16`).
ExecStart=/usr/bin/systemctl show %i --no-pager \
  --property=Id \
  --property=Result \
  --property=ExecMainCode \
  --property=ExecMainStatus \
  --property=InvocationID

SyslogIdentifier=hfcache-failure
SyslogLevel=err

# Modest hardening for a unit that runs one read-only systemctl. Proven rather
# than assumed, the same way the hardening policy's settings are: the hook is fired for real
# against a genuinely failed unit and its output read back out of the journal.
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=true
ProtectKernelTunables=true
ProtectKernelModules=true
ProtectKernelLogs=true
ProtectClock=true
RestrictSUIDSGID=true
RestrictNamespaces=true
LockPersonality=true
CapabilityBoundingSet=
AmbientCapabilities=
SystemCallArchitectures=native
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX
